insert into nutrient_guides (code, what_it_does, too_little_too_much, who_needs_more, tips, source_label, updated_at) values
('VIT_D','Vitamin D helps the body absorb calcium and keep bones and teeth strong. It also plays a part in how muscles and the immune system work.','Too little over a long time can leave bones weaker, and in children it can cause rickets. Too much almost always comes from high-dose supplements rather than food, and it can raise calcium in the blood to unhealthy levels.','People who spend little time in the sun, have darker skin, live far from the equator or are older may need more than others.','["Vitamin D is taken up better when the meal contains some fat.", "Oily fish, eggs and fortified milk or cereals are among the few food sources, and sunlight on the skin also makes it."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00'),
('VIT_E','Vitamin E works as an antioxidant, which means it helps protect cells from damage caused by unstable molecules. It also supports the immune system.','A true shortage is rare and usually linked to conditions that stop the body from absorbing fat. Too much from food is very unlikely, but high-dose supplements can interfere with blood clotting.','People whose bodies absorb fat poorly, for example because of certain digestive conditions, are the ones most likely to need more.','["Vitamin E dissolves in fat, so a little oil or a handful of nuts with a meal helps the body take it up.", "Sunflower seeds, almonds, plant oils and leafy greens are good sources."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00'),
('VIT_A','Vitamin A supports normal vision, especially in dim light, and helps keep skin and the lining of the airways healthy. It is also needed for a working immune system.','Too little can cause night blindness and make infections more likely, and it is a serious problem in parts of the world where diets are limited. Too much preformed vitamin A, mostly from supplements or large amounts of liver, can harm the liver and bones and is a particular risk in pregnancy.','People who eat very few vegetables, fruit, dairy or eggs, and those whose bodies absorb fat poorly, may need more.','["Orange and dark green vegetables such as carrots, sweet potato and spinach supply beta-carotene, which the body turns into vitamin A.", "Eating these with a little fat helps the body take up more of it."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00'),
('VIT_K','Vitamin K is needed to make proteins that let blood clot normally. It also helps the body build and maintain bone.','A shortage is uncommon in adults but can lead to bleeding that is slow to stop. Vitamin K from food has no known harmful upper level, though people taking blood-thinning medicines such as warfarin should keep their intake steady and follow their doctor''s advice.','Newborns are given vitamin K at birth because they start with low stores, and people with conditions that limit fat absorption may need more.','["Leafy greens such as kale, spinach and broccoli are the richest everyday sources.", "A little fat in the same meal helps the body take it up."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00'),
('THIAMIN','Thiamin, also called vitamin B1, helps the body turn carbohydrate into energy. It is also needed for healthy nerves and muscles.','A serious shortage causes beriberi, which affects nerves and the heart, and it is now seen mainly with heavy alcohol use or very limited diets. No harmful upper level has been set for thiamin from food or supplements.','People who drink alcohol heavily, older adults with poor appetite and people who have had weight-loss surgery may need more.','["Whole grains, pork, beans and lentils are good sources.", "Thiamin dissolves in water, so some is lost when food is boiled and the water is poured away."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00'),
('RIBOFLAVIN','Riboflavin, also called vitamin B2, helps the body release energy from food and keeps skin, eyes and the nervous system healthy. It is also involved in how the body uses other B vitamins.','Too little can cause a sore throat, cracked lips and skin changes, and it is uncommon where diets include dairy or eggs. No harmful upper level has been set, because the body passes extra amounts out in urine.','People who eat little dairy or meat, such as some vegans, and people who are pregnant or breastfeeding may need more.','["Milk, yogurt, eggs and fortified cereals are the main sources for many people.", "Riboflavin breaks down in light, so milk keeps its riboflavin better in cartons than in clear glass bottles."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00'),
('NIACIN','Niacin, also called vitamin B3, helps the body get energy from food and is needed for healthy skin, nerves and digestion. The body can also make some niacin from the protein it eats.','A serious shortage causes pellagra, which affects the skin, digestion and mind, and it is rare where protein intake is adequate. Large doses from supplements can cause flushing, an upset stomach and liver problems, while food alone rarely gives too much.','People who rely on a very limited diet, especially one low in protein, are the most likely to need more.','["Poultry, fish, peanuts and whole grains are good sources.", "Eating enough protein also helps, because the body can convert part of the amino acid tryptophan into niacin."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00'),
('VIT_B6','Vitamin B6 helps the body use protein and make brain chemicals and red blood cells. It is also involved in the way the immune system works.','Too little can cause anaemia, skin changes and low mood, and it usually occurs together with shortages of other B vitamins. Very high doses from supplements taken for a long time can damage nerves, while food alone rarely gives too much.','Older adults and people with certain kidney, liver or digestive conditions may need more.','["Chickpeas, fish, poultry, potatoes and bananas are good sources.", "Vitamin B6 is partly lost in cooking, so a mix of cooked and raw foods helps."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00'),
('FOLATE','Folate, also called vitamin B9, is needed to make DNA and new cells, including red blood cells. Getting enough before and early in pregnancy helps the baby''s brain and spinal cord form properly.','Too little can cause a type of anaemia and, in pregnancy, raises the risk of neural tube defects in the baby. The upper level applies to folic acid from supplements and fortified foods, and large amounts can hide a vitamin B12 shortage, while folate in natural foods is not a concern.','People who are pregnant or planning a pregnancy are advised to get more, and so may people who drink alcohol heavily or whose bodies absorb nutrients poorly.','["Leafy greens, beans, lentils and oranges are rich in folate.", "Folate is easily lost with long cooking in water, so steaming or eating some vegetables raw keeps more of it."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00'),
('VIT_B12','Vitamin B12 is needed to make red blood cells and DNA and to keep nerves working properly. It is found naturally almost only in foods that come from animals.','Too little can cause tiredness, anaemia and nerve problems, and it can take years to show because the body stores a good amount. No harmful upper level has been set, because the body takes up only what it needs from food.','People who eat no animal foods, older adults, and anyone whose body absorbs B12 poorly may need more or may need a fortified food or supplement.','["If you eat a vegetarian or vegan diet, look for fortified foods such as plant milks or cereals, or ask a health professional about a supplement.", "Fish, meat, eggs and dairy are the natural sources."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00'),
('VIT_C','Vitamin C helps the body make collagen, which holds skin, blood vessels and joints together, and it supports wound healing. It also works as an antioxidant and helps the immune system.','Too little over a long time causes scurvy, with tiredness, bleeding gums and slow healing, which is uncommon in people who eat fruit and vegetables. Very large doses from supplements can cause stomach upset and diarrhoea, while food alone rarely gives too much.','People who smoke need more, and so may people who eat very few fruit and vegetables.','["Vitamin C helps the body take up iron from plant foods, so pair beans or leafy greens with citrus fruit, peppers or tomatoes.", "Heat and long storage reduce vitamin C, so eating some fruit and vegetables raw helps."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00'),
('CALCIUM','Calcium is the main mineral in bones and teeth and also helps muscles contract, nerves send signals and blood clot. The body keeps the level in the blood steady by drawing on bone when intake is low.','Too little over many years can leave bones weaker and more likely to break. Too much, mostly from supplements, can cause constipation and kidney stones, and food alone rarely gives too much.','Women after menopause, older adults and people who avoid dairy without replacing it with other calcium sources may need more.','["Calcium is taken up better in smaller amounts through the day than in one large dose.", "Dairy, fortified plant milks, tofu set with calcium and canned fish with soft bones are good sources."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00'),
('MAGNESIUM','Magnesium takes part in hundreds of reactions in the body, including those that make energy and let muscles and nerves work. It also helps keep bones strong and the heartbeat steady.','Too little can cause tiredness, muscle cramps and, if it continues, changes in heart rhythm, though a true shortage is uncommon in healthy people. Magnesium from food is not a concern, but supplements can cause diarrhoea and stomach cramps.','People with digestive conditions, long-term heavy alcohol use or type 2 diabetes, and some older adults, may need more.','["Nuts, seeds, whole grains, beans and leafy greens are good sources.", "Refining grains removes much of their magnesium, so wholegrain versions give more."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00'),
('PHOSPHORUS','Phosphorus works with calcium to build bones and teeth and is part of the molecules that store and carry energy in cells. It also helps keep the body''s acid balance steady.','A shortage is rare because phosphorus is found in most foods. Very high intakes, mostly from additives and supplements, can upset the balance with calcium, and people with kidney disease may be told to limit it.','People with certain kidney, bone or digestive conditions, or who take some antacids for a long time, may be affected, and a health professional can advise them.','["Dairy, fish, poultry, beans and nuts are good sources.", "If you have kidney disease, ask your care team before changing foods that are high in phosphorus."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00'),
('POTASSIUM','Potassium helps nerves and muscles work, including the heart, and balances fluid in the body. Getting enough also helps keep blood pressure in a healthy range.','Too little can cause weakness, cramps and an irregular heartbeat, and it often follows vomiting, diarrhoea or some medicines. Too much from food is rarely a problem in healthy people, but people with kidney disease or on certain medicines should follow their doctor''s advice.','People who eat few fruit and vegetables, take water tablets or lose a lot of fluid may need more.','["Bananas, potatoes, beans, yogurt and leafy greens are good sources.", "Eating more potassium-rich foods while using less salt works well together for blood pressure."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00'),
('SODIUM','Sodium helps balance fluid in the body and is needed for nerves and muscles. Most of it in our diet comes from salt.','Too little is rare and is usually caused by illness or heavy sweating over a long time. Regularly eating much more than the guideline can raise blood pressure, which over the years increases the risk of heart disease and stroke.','People who sweat heavily or have certain medical conditions may need different amounts, and a health professional can advise them.','["Most sodium comes from packaged and restaurant food, so checking labels often helps more than using less salt at the table.", "Fresh fruit, vegetables, beans and unsalted nuts are naturally low in sodium."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00'),
('IRON','Iron is a main part of haemoglobin, the protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen around the body. It is also needed for muscles, energy and a healthy immune system.','Too little can lead to anaemia, with tiredness, weakness and trouble concentrating. Too much, mostly from supplements, can cause stomach upset and, in some people with an inherited condition, build up and harm organs.','People who menstruate, are pregnant, eat little meat or take part in a lot of endurance exercise may need more.','["Vitamin C helps the body take up iron from plant foods, so have fruit or peppers with beans, lentils or spinach.", "Tea and coffee with a meal can reduce how much iron is taken up, so it helps to have them between meals."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00'),
('ZINC','Zinc helps the immune system, wound healing and the sense of taste and smell. It is also needed to make proteins and DNA.','Too little can slow growth and healing and weaken the immune system. Too much over a long time, mostly from supplements, can lower copper levels and cause nausea, while food alone rarely gives too much.','People who eat little meat or seafood, such as vegetarians and vegans, and people with some digestive conditions may need more.','["Beans, nuts, seeds and whole grains contain zinc, though the body takes up less of it from plant foods.", "Soaking beans and grains, or choosing leavened bread, can help the body take up more."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00'),
('COPPER','Copper helps the body use iron and make red blood cells, connective tissue and brain chemicals. It also works with enzymes that protect cells from damage.','A shortage is rare but can cause anaemia and weak bones, and it can follow very high zinc intake from supplements. Too much copper from food is unlikely, but large supplement doses can harm the liver.','People who take high-dose zinc supplements, or whose bodies absorb nutrients poorly, may need more.','["Shellfish, nuts, seeds, whole grains and dark chocolate are good sources.", "If you take zinc supplements regularly, ask a health professional whether your copper intake is enough."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00'),
('SELENIUM','Selenium is part of enzymes that protect cells from damage and is needed for a healthy thyroid gland. It also supports the immune system.','A shortage is uncommon, but it can occur where soil is low in selenium and can affect the heart, thyroid and immune system. Too much, mostly from supplements, can cause hair and nail changes, a garlic-like breath and nerve problems.','People who live where the soil is low in selenium, have certain digestive conditions or receive kidney dialysis may need more.','["Brazil nuts are very rich in selenium, so one or two a day is plenty and more than that is not needed.", "Fish, eggs, meat and whole grains are other sources."]','NIH Office of Dietary Supplements',timestamp with time zone '2026-10-04 08:00:00+00');
